### Incident 2 — Bad Deployment (Wrong Image Tag)

**Scenario:** Developer pushed a deployment with a non-existent Docker image tag. Pods went into `ImagePullBackOff`.

**What I observed:**
```bash
kubectl get pods -n production -w

# Output:
# login-service-7dc5fd698c-9jmh8   0/1   ErrImagePull      0   13s
# login-service-7dc5fd698c-9jmh8   0/1   ImagePullBackOff  0   17s
# login-service-9695cf7b9-b7bfx    1/1   Running           0   90s  ← old pods kept serving
```

**Key learning:** Kubernetes rolling deployment kept old pods running while trying the new version — users were partially served, not fully down. This is why rolling deployments are the default strategy.

**Fix — Rollback in under 30 seconds:**
```bash
# Checked deployment history
kubectl rollout history deployment/login-service -n production

# Rolled back to last good version
kubectl rollout undo deployment/login-service -n production

# Watched recovery
kubectl rollout status deployment/login-service -n production
```

**Result:** Service fully restored within 90 seconds of rollback command.

